    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:7:3",
      "id": "lem-bounded-variation-gives-one-sided-dirichlet-integrability",
      "location": "Proof 1.2",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "The proof says that Bonnet's theorem 'therefore gives' ∫_1^B sin(y)g_N(y)dy=g_N(1)∫_1^xi sin(y)dy. The cited dependency thm-second-mean-value-theorem-for-integrals instead states ∫fg=f(a)∫_a^xi g+f(b)∫_xi^b g. Its second endpoint term does not generally vanish here: g_N(B) is nonzero for a general B=(2N+1)pi t. A separate reduction to the sharper form is needed but is not supplied.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
      "observed_sha256": "1592a946c96d713d7c446edcbd2b9a02466386dc718f4d29d0f96c70d7b7ed3d"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:7:4",
      "id": "thm-dirichlet-jordan-pointwise-convergence",
      "location": "Proof 1.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "The proof defines u_+(t)=f(x+t)-f(x+) and u_-(t)=f(x-t)-f(x-) on [0,delta], then says 'Also u_±(0)=0 by definition.' In fact u_+(0)=f(x)-f(x+) and u_-(0)=f(x)-f(x-), which need not be zero. For the one-periodic BV function f(n)=1 at integers n and f(y)=0 otherwise, at x=0 both one-sided limits are 0 but u_+(0)=u_-(0)=1. Thus the proof cannot apply the cited lemma in step 3.1, whose stated hypothesis requires u(0)=0, without redefining the endpoint values.",
      "severity": "fatal",
